00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
And then, the day came. 

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
We had been talking about it for weeks. 

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
We used to live in a colony of thirty families, on the bank of the Volta river. 

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.000
When I think about home, I think of a joyous crowd. 

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
I grew up surrounded by the constant chatter of my uncles and aunts. 

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:34.000
They used to pester us. The useless youth, they called us, eating, chittering and sunbathing on the riverbanks instead of getting ready. 

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.000
“When I was your age”, my aunt Afefa said, “I was already on my way to Europe!” 

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
What she didn’t know is, this was our way of getting ready. 

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.000
As sand martins, it is our number that gives us strength. 

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:59.000
How else would you expect a 14g bird to travel across 5000 kilometres? It’s madness, when you think about it. 

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:07.000
So we chatted to boost our confidence, bragging about who would be the first to cross the desert, who would fly the highest, the fastest. 

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
We chatted to forget how terrified we were. 

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Because we knew the odds, we knew them all too well. Three out of four of us would never see Europe. 

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.000
We couldn’t help looking at each other, wondering who it would be. 

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:37.000
So we made it last. We made provisions of sun, of sounds, of smells. We boosted our bodies and souls. We became stronger, as a team. 

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Because we knew that, as much as we would miss home, we simply had no choice. We had to go. 
